te to."],[947,"The kit's personas (personas/\\*.json): \\{id, title, summary, datasets, addons, visible}."],[1070,"The kit's personas (personas/\\*.json): \\{id, title, summary, datasets, addons, visible}."],[460,"The Marimo versions"],[1054,"The marketplace's add-ons as `exakit marketplace --list --json` reports them: \\{id, name, about, status,\ninstalled, version, url}, and `offered`: False for one the kit's catalog does not offer yet (its\nmarketplace/catalog.json says \"visible\": false, so no marketplace screen lists it)."],[791,"The marketplace's add-ons as `exakit marketplace --list --json` reports them: \\{id, name, about, status, installed, version, url}, and `offered`: False for one the kit's catalog does not offer yet (its marketplace/catalog.json says \"visible\": false, so no marketplace screen lists it)."],[44,"The message is a `DescriptorNotApproved` error. Explore, Ask and Share do not read a draft. Open `00_quickstart` and approve the map. See Review and approve the column map."],[239,"The ML Engineer's handoff is a different thing: one JSON file with the feature view, the target and a reproducible split, written by `nb.write_handoff(connection, dataset)` to `~/.exasol-starter-kit/handoffs/`. A stale one is refused, and `nb.handoff_is_stale` names what changed."],[1340,"The MLflow server this kernel records runs to: `tracking_uri` (empty: the kit's own MLflow, or what the shell\nset); `login=True` asks for the server's username and password (hidden). Prints where runs go, never a password."],[999,"The MLflow server this kernel records runs to: `tracking_uri` (empty: the kit's own MLflow, or what the shell set); `login=True` asks for the server's username and password (hidden)."],[66,"The model is yours"],[1074,"The model lab: nb.model_lab - the Data Scientist chooses the task, the model and the split."],[363,"The model's id"],[370,"The model's id and address can also live in `~/.exasol-starter-kit/exakit.env`. Provider recipes are in `MODEL-PROVIDERS.md`, copied beside the notebooks."],[395,"The new helper now has an entry in its module's reference page and a row in All commands. `nb.docs(nb.your_helper)` opens it once the site is rebuilt."],[191,"The next **version** is written to the kit's store on this computer, under `~/.exasol-starter-kit/descriptors/`."],[1480,"The nine steps"],[322,"The notebook then reads up to 20,000 rows, trains a small baseline model on the category, the entity and the month, scores it on rows it has not seen, and shows what it leans on. `nb.note_model` says in plain words what the score means."],[565,"The notebook's Dash Server tool adapter; each call is an MCP tool call."],[837,"The notebook's Dash Server tool adapter; each call is an MCP tool call."],[1469,"The notebook's own cells sit under **The code behind this page**. In the kit's JupyterLab they are hidden until you choose **Show all the notebook's code cells** at the foot of a card's Code view. In Marimo the cells open with their code visible."],[1476,"The notebooks and the nine steps"],[733,"The notebooks' documentation, opened from a cell: nb.docs()."],[1330,"The optional settings, set from a notebook cell: your model and Langfuse (use_model), the MLflow server (use_mlflow)."],[155,"The outcome to predict"],[339,"The password is asked in a hidden box. It is never part of the code."],[1479,"The path"],[1239,"The picker is the descriptor itself: one dropdown per column with the eight roles (key, time, measure, category, flag,\ntext, identifier, other), set to the role the rules drafted and labelled with the evidence that chose it; then one\ndropdown per slot - the columns the notebooks use (key, time, text, target, category, entity) - set to the drafted\ncolumn; then an Approve button. In JupyterLab it is\nipywidgets; in Marimo the review itself is a `mo.ui.dictionary` of `mo.ui.dropdown` with a `mo.ui.run_button`, so\n`ds = review.dataset()` runs again when the button is clicked and stops cleanly (`mo.stop`) until then; in a plain\nPython session `review.approve({\"COLUMN\": \"role\", ...})` is the same path."],[82,"The profile of the table"],[1166,"The proposal for `ds`'s table: its types, its cleaning and the CREATE VIEW, built from the data and the\ndescriptor. Only SELECTs run; nothing is created until `create()`."],[995,"The proposal for `ds`'s table: its types, its cleaning and the CREATE VIEW, built from the data and the descriptor."],[885,"The proposal for one wide view."],[1282,"The proposal for one wide view. `joins` lists each table it adds and how; `sql` is what create() runs."],[1328,"The proposal: `main` (a dataset, or a table name) with every table its links reach."],[997,"The proposal."],[1168,"The proposal. `changes` and `cleaning` say what the view does differently from the table, `sql` is the\nCREATE VIEW that `create()` runs - nothing has run before that."],[353,"The quickest way"],[543,"The quickstarts' shared steps (check_connection, list_schemas, load_sample, load_source, ...) are in quickstart.py;\nthe ML Engineer's checks (check_container, check_mlflow, check_bucket) in checks.py."],[680,"The role review (role_picker) speaks the five roles of earlier descriptors - entity, category, date, label, text - so\n`roles`, `evidence`, `missing` and `set(role, column)` read and write the matching slots: date is the time\nslot and label the target."],[705,"The role review's correction: `role` (one of the five) to `column`, or empty with `reason`."],[192,"The roles are published as column comments on a view in the `EXAKIT_SEMANTIC` schema. If publishing fails, the approval still stands and the message says why."],[654,"The roles no column fits, each `{role, reason}`; the notebooks skip what depends on them with the reason."],[696,"The roles no column plays, each with the reason the empty slot gives."],[555,"The rows come over the normal connection rather than pyexasol's HTTP export, which needs the database to connect\nback to this machine - not a given through the local VM or Podman."],[609,"The rules are the kit's descriptor rules (`descriptor_rules`, shipped with this package: the 0.2 kit has no Python\npackage to import them from). It is a copy of tools/descriptor_rules.py, which tools/dataset_check.py uses, and\ntests/test_notebook_contents.py holds the two byte-identical. Nothing is guessed: a descriptor that is invalid, or\nnames a table or column the database lacks, stops the notebook with the reason."],[965,"The schema as found (a draft) or as approved: `doc` holds its tables and links."],[1307,"The schema as found (a draft) or as approved: `doc` holds its tables and links."],[54,"The schema is not found"],[206,"The schema map shows the tables that link to this one, with the evidence for each link. A link adds the other table's columns without adding rows."],[231,"The schema map, when there is one"],[1280,"The schema of `dataset_or_schema` (a dataset, or a schema name) as found now: its tables, rows and keys, and\nevery many-to-one link between them with its evidence. A live source's schema is not surveyed (its tables are\nqueried in place, every count a round trip)."],[853,"The schema of `dataset_or_schema` (a dataset, or a schema name) as found now: its tables, rows and keys, and every many-to-one link between them with its evidence."],[586,"The semantic map of the database: every table by origin (sample, local, source) with its rows, columns,\ncolumn-map state and roles, then the links between tables - declared, found by an approved schema map, or a\nshared key-like column name across two tables (marked possible). Reads metadata only; returns a DataMap that\nshows itself as a table in a notebook. `include_kit` keeps the kit's own schemas in."],[843,"The semantic map of the database: every table by origin (sample, local, source) with its rows, columns, column-map state and roles, then the links between tables - declared, found by an approved schema map, or a shared key-like column name across two tables (marked possible